************** G E N E R A L   I N F O R M A T I O N *************9437 1451  101
                                                                  9437 1451  102
This evaluated data file is based primarily on the nuclear model  9437 1451  103
code Talys [kon07], version 1.4 and on existing resonance         9437 1451  104
parameter tables. It is part of a large collection of isotopic    9437 1451  105
evaluations, all created by running Talys with either default or  9437 1451  106
adjusted input parameters. This means that the data in this       9437 1451  107
evaluation have been tested in detail for some isotopes against   9437 1451  108
individual experimental data, while for other isotopes it is      9437 1451  109
only as good as the global quality of Talys at the present moment.9437 1451  110
The mutual quality of all individual evaluations is however       9437 1451  111
consistent: The same set of nuclear models is used and, equally   9437 1451  112
important, the same ENDF-6 formatting procedures for each isotope.9437 1451  113
The resulting data file provides a complete representation of     9437 1451  114
nuclear data needed for transport, damage, heating, radioactivity,9437 1451  115
and shielding applications over the incident neutron energy range 9437 1451  116
from 1.0E-11 to 200 MeV.                                          9437 1451  117
                                                                  9437 1451  118
For the resonance range we have adopted the best possible         9437 1451  119
resonance parameters from available numerical tables or from an   9437 1451  120
existing library.                                                 9437 1451  121
                                                                  9437 1451  122
All transport data for neutrons, charged particles, photons and   9437 1451  123
residual nuclides are filed using a combination of MF1,2,3,4,5,6, 9437 1451  124
12,14,15. This includes cross sections, angular distributions,    9437 1451  125
double-differential spectra, discrete and continuum photon        9437 1451  126
production cross sections, and residual production (activation)   9437 1451  127
cross sections. Moreover, isomeric production data are stored in  9437 1451  128
MF8,9,10. This evaluation can thus be used as both transport      9437 1451  129
and activation library. The data file has been created            9437 1451  130
automatically using the ENDF-6 format generator TEFAL.            9437 1451  131
                                                                  9437 1451  132
A complete set of covariance data is included, which is stored    9437 1451  133
in MF32,33,34.                                                    9437 1451  134
                                                                  9437 1451  135
##### ORIGIN                                                      9437 1451  136
                                                                  9437 1451  137
Resonance parameters (MF2/MT151): Best data from existing sets    9437 1451  138
All other data < 200 MeV        : Produced with Talys code        9437 1451  139
                                                                  9437 1451  140
*************************** T H E O R Y **************************9437 1451  141
                                                                  9437 1451  142
Talys is a computer code system for the prediction and analysis   9437 1451  143
of nuclear reactions. Talys simulates reactions that involve      9437 1451  144
neutrons, gamma-rays, protons, deuterons, tritons, helions and    9437 1451  145
alpha-particles, in the 1 keV - 200 MeV energy range and for      9437 1451  146
target nuclides of mass 12 and heavier. This is achieved by       9437 1451  147
implementing a suite of nuclear reaction models into a single     9437 1451  148
code system. It enables to evaluate nuclear reactions from        9437 1451  149
the unresolved resonance region up to intermediate energies. This 9437 1451  150
evaluation is based on a theoretical analysis that utilizes the   9437 1451  151
optical model, compound nucleus statistical theory, fission,      9437 1451  152
direct reactions and pre-equilibrium processes, in combination    9437 1451  153
with databases and models for nuclear structure. The following    9437 1451  154
output of Talys is stored in this data file:                      9437 1451  155
                                                                  9437 1451  156
- Total, elastic and non-elastic cross sections                   9437 1451  157
- Elastic scattering angular distributions                        9437 1451  158
- Inelastic cross sections to discrete states                     9437 1451  159
- Inelastic scattering angular distributions to discrete states   9437 1451  160
- Exclusive channel cross sections, e.g. (n,g), (n,2n), (n,np),.. 9437 1451  161
- Exclusive channel energy spectra                                9437 1451  162
- Exclusive channel double-differential spectra                   9437 1451  163
- Exclusive gamma production for discrete states and continuum    9437 1451  164
- Isomeric and ground state cross sections                        9437 1451  165
- Residual production cross sections                              9437 1451  166
- Recoil data                                                     9437 1451  167
- Total particle cross sections, e.g. (n,xn), (n,xp),..           9437 1451  168
- Total particle energy spectra                                   9437 1451  169
- Total particle double-differential spectra                      9437 1451  170
                                                                  9437 1451  171
Here follows a short description of the used nuclear models:      9437 1451  172
                                                                  9437 1451  173
##### OPTICAL MODEL                                               9437 1451  174
                                                                  9437 1451  175
All optical model calculations are performed by ECIS-06 [ray94],  9437 1451  176
in Talys used as a subroutine. The default optical model          9437 1451  177
potentials (OMP) used are the local and global parameterizations  9437 1451  178
of Koning and Delaroche [kon03]. These are phenomenological OMPs  9437 1451  179
for neutrons and protons which in principle are valid over the    9437 1451  180
1 keV - 200 MeV energy range, though the low energy boundary of   9437 1451  181
validity may differ from nucleus to nucleus. For neutrons and     9437 1451  182
protons, the used parameterization is given in Eq. (7) of         9437 1451  183
[kon03]. Revised versions of these potentials (unpublished) are   9437 1451  184
used in the case of deformed nuclides.                            9437 1451  185
To calculate the transmission coefficients and reaction           9437 1451  186
cross sections for deuterons, tritons, helions and alpha          9437 1451  187
particles, we use OMPs that are directly derived from our nucleon 9437 1451  188
potentials using Watanabe's folding approach.                     9437 1451  189
                                                                  9437 1451  190
##### DIRECT REACTIONS                                            9437 1451  191
                                                                  9437 1451  192
The built-in ECIS-06 is used for DWBA or coupled-channels         9437 1451  193
calculations for rotational or vibrational (or a combination of   9437 1451  194
these) nuclides, depending on the information present in the      9437 1451  195
nuclear structure database of Talys.                              9437 1451  196
In addition, a macroscopic, phenomenological model to describe    9437 1451  197
giant resonances in the inelastic channel is used. For each       9437 1451  198
multipolarity an energy weighted sum rule applies and a DWBA      9437 1451  199
calculation with ECIS-06 is performed for each giant resonance    9437 1451  200
state. The cross section is then spread over the continuum with a 9437 1451  201
Gaussian distribution.                                            9437 1451  202
                                                                  9437 1451  203
##### COMPOUND NUCLEUS                                            9437 1451  204
                                                                  9437 1451  205
For binary compound nucleus reactions we use the model of         9437 1451  206
Moldauer, i.e. the Hauser-Feshbach model corrected for width      9437 1451  207
fluctuations. The transmission coefficients have been generated   9437 1451  208
with the aforementioned OMPs and the full j,l-dependence of the   9437 1451  209
transmission coefficients in the Hauser-Feshbach model is used.   9437 1451  210
For each nucleus that can be reached through a binary reaction,   9437 1451  211
several discrete levels and a continuum described by level        9437 1451  212
densities are included simultaneously as competing channels.      9437 1451  213
                                                                  9437 1451  214
The compound nucleus angular distributions are calculated with    9437 1451  215
the Blatt-Biedenharn formalism, leading to compound nucleus       9437 1451  216
Legendre coefficients that are added to their direct              9437 1451  217
counterparts. For multiple compound emission, i.e. emitted        9437 1451  218
particles after the binary emission, we use the Hauser-Feshbach   9437 1451  219
model. Again, for each residual nucleus several discrete states   9437 1451  220
are included as well as a continuum described by level densities. 9437 1451  221
Multiple compound emission is continued until all reaction        9437 1451  222
channels are closed and the population distribution of all        9437 1451  223
residual nuclides is depleted, through gamma decay, until they    9437 1451  224
end up in the ground state or in an isomer.                       9437 1451  225
                                                                  9437 1451  226
For the level density, we take the Constant Temperature Model     9437 1451  227
with level density parameters as given in [kon08]. This employs   9437 1451  228
the temperature law at low energies and a Fermi gas expression at 9437 1451  229
high energies, and takes into account the damping of shell        9437 1451  230
effects at high excitation energy. We have obtained the level     9437 1451  231
density parameters from a simultaneous fit to all experimental    9437 1451  232
discrete levels and D0 values from the RIPL library [rip09].      9437 1451  233
                                                                  9437 1451  234
Gamma-ray transmission coefficients are generated with the        9437 1451  235
Kopecky-Uhl generalized Lorentzian for strength functions, with   9437 1451  236
giant dipole resonance parameters taken from the RIPL library     9437 1451  237
[rip09], and normalized with experimental radiative widths.       9437 1451  238
                                                                  9437 1451  239
##### PRE-EQUILIBRIUM REACTIONS                                   9437 1451  240
                                                                  9437 1451  241
For pre-equilibrium reactions, which become important for         9437 1451  242
incident energies above about 10 MeV, we use the two-component    9437 1451  243
exciton model [kon04], in which the neutron or proton types of    9437 1451  244
particles and holes are followed throughout the reaction. For     9437 1451  245
energies above 20 MeV, multiple pre-equilibrium emission up to    9437 1451  246
any order of particle emission was included in the calculations.  9437 1451  247
A parameterization for the squared matrix element is used that is 9437 1451  248
valid for the whole energy range of this evaluation.              9437 1451  249
                                                                  9437 1451  250
For deuterons, tritons, helions and alpha-particles, an extra     9437 1451  251
contribution was added from the pick/up and knock-out reaction    9437 1451  252
model by Kalbach [kal05].                                         9437 1451  253
                                                                  9437 1451  254
For photons, the model of Akkermans and Gruppelaar [akk85] was    9437 1451  255
applied, to simulate the direct and semi-direct capture           9437 1451  256
processes.                                                        9437 1451  257
                                                                  9437 1451  258
The angular distribution systematics by Kalbach [kal88] were used 9437 1451  259
to describe the angular distributions for all continuum           9437 1451  260
particles. An isotopic distribution for photons was adopted.      9437 1451  261
                                                                  9437 1451  262
***************** F I L E   I N F O R M A T I O N ****************9437 1451  263
                                                                  9437 1451  264
##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    9437 1451  265
                                                                  9437 1451  266
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       9437 1451  267
                                                                  9437 1451  268
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          9437 1451  269
                                                                  9437 1451  270
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   9437 1451  271
                                                                  9437 1451  272
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 9437 1451  273
                                                                  9437 1451  274
If present, we adopt the resonance parameters from an existing    9437 1451  275
table or library. The choice of library is made, for each isotope 9437 1451  276
separately, on basis of completeness, date of the evaluation and  9437 1451  277
used representation. If not present, the potential scattering     9437 1451  278
radius, calculated from the optical model, is written to MF2.     9437 1451  279
The default is to take the resonances from a revised version of   9437 1451  280
the Atlas of Neutron Resonances, 5th edition [mug06].             9437 1451  281
                                                                  9437 1451  282
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                9437 1451  283
                                                                  9437 1451  284
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       9437 1451  285
calculated with Talys. If the maximal cross section in an         9437 1451  286
excitation function over the whole energy range does not exceed   9437 1451  287
1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not included at all. Cross sections     9437 1451  288
lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have no physical meaning and   9437 1451  289
are set to zero. We present only the most important reactions     9437 1451  290
explicitly with a separate MT-number. The minor reaction channels 9437 1451  291
are also present but they are stored in MT5 and can be            9437 1451  292
reproduced in combination with MF6. All MT numbers extend up to   9437 1451  293
the highest incident energy.                                      9437 1451  294
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:9437 1451  295
                                                                  9437 1451  296
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  9437 1451  297
                                                                  9437 1451  298
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    9437 1451  299
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     9437 1451  300
                                                                  9437 1451  301
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     9437 1451  302
                                                                  9437 1451  303
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    9437 1451  304
total cross section.                                              9437 1451  305
                                                                  9437 1451  306
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            9437 1451  307
                                                                  9437 1451  308
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    9437 1451  309
                                                                  9437 1451  310
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        9437 1451  311
                                                                  9437 1451  312
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  9437 1451  313
                                                                  9437 1451  314
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           9437 1451  315
                                                                  9437 1451  316
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   9437 1451  317
MT-number. As the incident energy increases, the cross section    9437 1451  318
of MT5 increases as more reaction channels are no longer stored   9437 1451  319
under a specific MT number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be     9437 1451  320
combined with MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross         9437 1451  321
sections particle production cross sections and                   9437 1451  322
(double-)differential cross sections.                             9437 1451  323
                                                                  9437 1451  324
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 9437 1451  325
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 9437 1451  326
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 9437 1451  327
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 9437 1451  328
- MT51-70  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-20th excited state      9437 1451  329
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       9437 1451  330
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              9437 1451  331
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  9437 1451  332
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  9437 1451  333
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  9437 1451  334
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  9437 1451  335
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  9437 1451  336
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       9437 1451  337
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        9437 1451  338
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        9437 1451  339
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        9437 1451  340
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        9437 1451  341
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        9437 1451  342
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        9437 1451  343
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        9437 1451  344
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       9437 1451  345
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        9437 1451  346
                                                                  9437 1451  347
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           9437 1451  348
                                                                  9437 1451  349
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    9437 1451  350
discrete levels.                                                  9437 1451  351
                                                                  9437 1451  352
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         9437 1451  353
                                                                  9437 1451  354
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  9437 1451  355
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   9437 1451  356
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      9437 1451  357
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  9437 1451  358
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      9437 1451  359
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         9437 1451  360
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   9437 1451  361
on an angular grid.                                               9437 1451  362
                                                                  9437 1451  363
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       9437 1451  364
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        9437 1451  365
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         9437 1451  366
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         9437 1451  367
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         9437 1451  368
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        9437 1451  369
                                                                  9437 1451  370
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     9437 1451  371
                                                                  9437 1451  372
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     9437 1451  373
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   9437 1451  374
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  9437 1451  375
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             9437 1451  376
All data are generated with Talys.                                9437 1451  377
                                                                  9437 1451  378
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   9437 1451  379
                                                                  9437 1451  380
MT5 contains the production yields of particles and residual      9437 1451  381
products. It also contains the secondary energy-angle             9437 1451  382
distributions for all particles and photons which are not         9437 1451  383
represented by explicit MT numbers. First, the yields for         9437 1451  384
neutrons are given for the whole energy range. Next, on a         9437 1451  385
secondary energy grid the relative emission spectra are given     9437 1451  386
together with the parameters for the Kalbach systematics for      9437 1451  387
angular distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for    9437 1451  388
discrete states have been broadened and added to the continuum    9437 1451  389
spectra. This procedure is repeated for protons, deuterons,       9437 1451  390
tritons, Helium-3, alpha particles and photons. Finally, the      9437 1451  391
residual production yields are given per final product. All these 9437 1451  392
yields and relative distributions can be multiplied with the      9437 1451  393
cross sections given in MF3/MT5 to get the production cross       9437 1451  394
sections and (double-)differential cross sections.                9437 1451  395
                                                                  9437 1451  396
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     9437 1451  397
                                                                  9437 1451  398
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  9437 1451  399
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 9437 1451  400
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          9437 1451  401
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       9437 1451  402
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 9437 1451  403
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     9437 1451  404
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  9437 1451  405
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      9437 1451  406
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         9437 1451  407
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             9437 1451  408
                                                                  9437 1451  409
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   9437 1451  410
followed:                                                         9437 1451  411
                                                                  9437 1451  412
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9437 1451  413
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9437 1451  414
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9437 1451  415
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     9437 1451  416
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9437 1451  417
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9437 1451  418
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9437 1451  419
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9437 1451  420
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9437 1451  421
                                                                  9437 1451  422
-----                                                             9437 1451  423
                                                                  9437 1451  424
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 9437 1451  425
                                                                  9437 1451  426
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    9437 1451  427
for isomeric versus ground state production can be found, i.e.    9437 1451  428
for each MT number it points to either MF6, MF9 or MF10.          9437 1451  429
                                                                  9437 1451  430
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  9437 1451  431
                                                                  9437 1451  432
MF9 is used for non-threshold reactions.                          9437 1451  433
All data are generated with Talys. Final states with a lifetime   9437 1451  434
that exceeds 1 second are treated as isomer.                      9437 1451  435
                                                                  9437 1451  436
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 9437 1451  437
                                                                  9437 1451  438
MF10 is used for threshold reactions.                             9437 1451  439
All data are generated with Talys. Final states with a lifetime   9437 1451  440
that exceeds 1 second are treated as isomer.                      9437 1451  441
                                                                  9437 1451  442
##### MF12: PHOTOPRODUCTION YIELD DATA                            9437 1451  443
                                                                  9437 1451  444
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:9437 1451  445
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   9437 1451  446
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   9437 1451  447
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   9437 1451  448
  given                                                           9437 1451  449
                                                                  9437 1451  450
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          9437 1451  451
                                                                  9437 1451  452
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  9437 1451  453
                                                                  9437 1451  454
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 9437 1451  455
                                                                  9437 1451  456
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         9437 1451  457
                                                                  9437 1451  458
